---
status: 'published';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Web Builders Guide - Choose the approach that fits"
  description="A practical guide to website builders, custom websites, content management systems, and drag-and-drop platforms."
>
  <!-- Hero with background image -->
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
    <div class="absolute inset-0">
      <img
        src="/images/guides/gold-circles.webp"
        alt=""
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
        class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-40 dark:opacity-50"
      />
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-b from-white/85 via-white/60 to-white/20 dark:from-gray-950/85 dark:via-gray-950/70 dark:to-gray-950/40"
      >
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="relative z-10">
      <div
        class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-24 md:py-32 text-center"
      >
        <p
          class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300 mb-2"
        >
          Guides
        </p>
        <h1
          class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-serif font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4"
        >
          Web Builders Guide
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A website platform is a means, not a destination. The right choice
          depends on what you are building, how often it will change, who will
          maintain it, and how much flexibility you need over time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section: Start with fit -->
  <section
    class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-950 border-t border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800"
  >
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 md:py-16">
      <h2
        class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4"
      >
        Start with fit, not favorites.
      </h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 max-w-3xl mb-4">
        Most modern website tools can produce a good-looking site. The
        differences show up later: how easy it is to update, how much control
        you have, what it costs to maintain, and whether the platform still
        feels right as your work evolves.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 max-w-3xl">
        I do not start by forcing every project into one builder. I start with
        the work, the people maintaining the site, and the kind of experience
        the website needs to support.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Decorative band -->
  <div class="relative">
    <img
      src="/images/guides/dots-blue-yellow-tws-thin.webp"
      alt=""
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      class="w-full h-24 object-cover opacity-85 dark:opacity-70"
    />
    <div
      class="absolute inset-0 ring-1 ring-stone-200/70 dark:ring-stone-800/70 pointer-events-none"
    >
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section: Three broad approaches -->
  <section class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 md:py-16">
      <h2
        class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4"
      >
        Three broad approaches
      </h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 max-w-3xl mb-6">
        The names of the platforms matter less than the kind of ownership
        experience they create.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <article
          class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
        >
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2">
            Custom websites
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
            Built specifically around the project. A good fit when structure,
            performance, expression, and long-term flexibility matter.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
            Examples: Astro, custom WordPress, or other developer-led builds.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article
          class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
        >
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2">
            Content-managed websites
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
            Designed so content can be updated through a browser-based editor. A
            good fit when publishing, updating, or collaboration matter.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
            Examples: WordPress and similar content management systems.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article
          class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
        >
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2">
            Drag-and-drop builders
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 mb-3">
            Fast to launch and easier to edit directly. A good fit when
            simplicity, speed, and self-sufficiency matter more than deep
            customization.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
            Examples: Squarespace, Wix, Webflow, Framer, and similar builders.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section: Tradeoffs -->
  <section
    class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-950 border-t border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800"
  >
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 md:py-16">
      <h2
        class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4"
      >
        Strengths and tradeoffs
      </h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 max-w-3xl mb-6">
        No platform is best for every situation. The goal is to understand what
        each path makes easier and what it may make harder later.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <article
          class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
        >
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-3">
            Custom websites
          </h3>
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
            Strengths
          </h4>
          <ul
            class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 space-y-1 mb-4"
          >
            <li>High control over structure and design.</li>
            <li>Strong performance when built well.</li>
            <li>Good fit for crafted, stable, expressive sites.</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
            Tradeoffs
          </h4>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 space-y-1">
            <li>Often requires more technical involvement.</li>
            <li>Some edits may need a developer or careful workflow.</li>
            <li>Not always ideal for frequent non-technical editing.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article
          class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
        >
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-3">
            Content-managed websites
          </h3>
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
            Strengths
          </h4>
          <ul
            class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 space-y-1 mb-4"
          >
            <li>Good for publishing and ongoing updates.</li>
            <li>Browser-based editing can be approachable.</li>
            <li>Flexible enough for many kinds of projects.</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
            Tradeoffs
          </h4>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 space-y-1">
            <li>Benefits from occasional maintenance.</li>
            <li>Plugins and themes need thoughtful choices.</li>
            <li>More flexibility can also create more complexity.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article
          class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
        >
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-3">
            Drag-and-drop builders
          </h3>
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
            Strengths
          </h4>
          <ul
            class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 space-y-1 mb-4"
          >
            <li>Fast to launch.</li>
            <li>Lower technical barrier.</li>
            <li>Hosting and maintenance are mostly handled for you.</li>
          </ul>
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-1">
            Tradeoffs
          </h4>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 space-y-1">
            <li>Less control than a custom-built site.</li>
            <li>You usually work within the platform's ecosystem.</li>
            <li>Moving later may require rebuilding parts of the site.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section: How to choose -->
  <section class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-950">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 md:py-16">
      <h2
        class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4"
      >
        How to choose
      </h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 max-w-3xl mb-6">
        The right tool usually becomes clearer when you think about control,
        editing, complexity, and future changes.
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <article
          class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
        >
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2">
            Choose more control when...
          </h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
            <li>The site should feel highly tailored to the work.</li>
            <li>Performance, structure, or longevity matter.</li>
            <li>
              You want something more distinctive than a standard template.
            </li>
            <li>You are comfortable getting help for some changes.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article
          class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
        >
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2">
            Choose easier self-editing when...
          </h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
            <li>You want to change text, images, or sections yourself.</li>
            <li>The site will be updated often by non-technical people.</li>
            <li>Speed and convenience matter more than deep customization.</li>
            <li>You prefer an all-in-one platform with fewer moving parts.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section: My role -->
  <section
    class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-950 border-t border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800"
  >
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 md:py-16">
      <h2
        class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4"
      >
        Where I can help
      </h2>
      <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 max-w-3xl mb-4">
        I can help clarify the project, shape the structure, compare practical
        options, and build when the fit is right. I am comfortable working with
        custom sites, WordPress, and learning or adapting to simpler builder
        platforms when they are the better choice.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 max-w-3xl">
        Some projects need deeper development, complex ecommerce, advanced
        memberships, or custom apps. In those cases, I can help define the
        project clearly and involve or recommend a specialist.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section: Common questions -->
  <section
    id="faq"
    class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-950 border-t border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800"
  >
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 md:py-16">
      <h2
        class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4"
      >
        Common questions
      </h2>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <article
          class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
        >
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2">
            Can I edit it myself?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
            Usually, yes. The question is how much you want to edit and how
            often. Some platforms are built around direct self-editing; others
            offer more control but require a more careful workflow.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article
          class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
        >
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2">
            Which is best for search?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
            Clear structure, useful content, readable pages, performance, and
            accurate business information matter more than the builder name.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article
          class="rounded-lg border border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800 p-6"
        >
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-2">
            What if I choose the wrong platform?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
            It is usually fixable, but switching later can take work. That is
            why it helps to think about ownership, editing, and future needs
            before building.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section: Next steps -->
  <section
    class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-950 border-t border-stone-200 dark:border-stone-800"
  >
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-4">
        <a
          href="/guides"
          class="inline-block bg-blue-700 text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-lg font-semibold hover:bg-blue-800 transition dark:bg-blue-500 dark:hover:bg-blue-400"
        >
          Back to Guides
        </a>
        <a
          href="/guides/planning"
          class="inline-block border border-blue-700 text-blue-700 px-6 py-3 rounded-lg font-semibold hover:bg-blue-50 transition dark:border-blue-300 dark:text-blue-300 dark:hover:bg-blue-900/30"
        >
          Open Planning
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="text-center text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 mt-3">
        The best platform is the one that fits the work and the people who will
        live with it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
